🔐 What Is SNI?

SNI (Server Name Indication) is a TLS extension that allows a client to tell the server which hostname it wants to connect to during the TLS handshake — before the HTTPS connection is fully established.

It solves a fundamental HTTPS problem.
🧠 The Problem SNI Solves

Imagine a server hosting:

    example.com

    test.com

Both use HTTPS (port 443).

With plain TLS (without SNI):

    The server must send a certificate

    But it doesn’t yet know which hostname the client wants

    So it can only send one certificate

That means:

    ❌ Only one HTTPS site per IP address (historically)

This was a big limitation.
🚀 The Solution: SNI

With SNI:

During the TLS handshake, the client sends:

"I want example.com"

inside the ClientHello message.

Now the server can:

    Select the correct certificate

    Select the correct server block

    Continue TLS properly

🧠 Where It Happens (Very Important)

Order of events:

1️⃣ TCP connection
2️⃣ TLS handshake begins
3️⃣ ClientHello (contains SNI hostname)
4️⃣ Server selects certificate based on SNI
5️⃣ TLS handshake completes
6️⃣ HTTP request is sent

⚠ Notice:

SNI happens before HTTP
There is no Host header yet.
HTTP hasn’t started.
🏗 Example in NGINX

server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name example.com;

    ssl_certificate example.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key example.key;
}

server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name test.com;

    ssl_certificate test.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key test.key;
}

When browser connects to:

https://test.com

Browser sends SNI = test.com

NGINX selects:

    Second server block

    test.com certificate

🧠 Difference: SNI vs Host Header
Feature	SNI	Host Header
Protocol Layer	TLS	HTTP
Happens when?	During handshake	After TLS
Used for?	Selecting certificate	Selecting virtual host
Mandatory?	Not required by TLS spec	Required in HTTP/1.1
🔥 Why This Matters for You (42 Webserv)

For your project:

    You are not implementing TLS (unless bonus)

    So you don't implement SNI

    You only implement HTTP

    So you match using the Host header only

But conceptually:

HTTPS server selection = SNI first
HTTP server selection = Host header

If one day you implement HTTPS:

You must:

    Accept TLS handshake

    Parse ClientHello

    Extract SNI

    Choose certificate

    Then parse HTTP

That is significantly harder.
🌍 Real-World Impact

Without SNI:

    Each HTTPS site needed a dedicated IP

With SNI:

    Millions of domains share same IP

    Shared hosting works over HTTPS

Modern browsers all support SNI.
🎯 Final One-Sentence Definition

    SNI is a TLS extension that allows a client to specify the hostname it wants to connect to during the TLS handshake so the server can select the correct certificate.

