🧠 If IP Is NOT Specified in listen

Example:

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com;
}

What does this mean?

It means:

listen 0.0.0.0:80;

On IPv4 systems.

And usually also:

listen [::]:80;

for IPv6 (depending on system config).
🌍 What Is 0.0.0.0?

0.0.0.0 means:

    Bind to ALL available network interfaces

So if your machine has:

    127.0.0.1

    192.168.1.10

    10.0.0.5

NGINX will accept connections on port 80 for all of them.
🧠 What Happens Internally

If IP is not specified:

NGINX creates a socket:

bind(INADDR_ANY, port)

Which means:

Any IP + that port

So step 1 (IP:port matching) becomes:

Port match only

Because IP is wildcarded.
🔥 Important Interaction Example

Config:

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com;
}

server {
    listen 127.0.0.1:80;
    server_name test.com;
}

What happens?

NGINX behavior:

    First server binds to 0.0.0.0:80

    Second tries to bind to 127.0.0.1:80

⚠ This would normally cause a conflict.

In practice:

NGINX merges compatible listen directives internally if possible.

But conceptually:

0.0.0.0:80 overlaps with 127.0.0.1:80

So they belong to the same port group.
🧠 For Your Webserv Implementation

You need to treat:

listen 80;

as:

ip = "0.0.0.0"
port = 80

And that means:

    It matches ANY incoming destination IP on that port.

So when a connection arrives:

If your socket was created with:

sin_addr.s_addr = INADDR_ANY;

You cannot distinguish which IP was targeted.

The OS gives you the connection, but you don't know which local IP the client used — unless you use:

getsockname()

(which you're allowed to use 👌)
🧠 Real NGINX Matching Logic

Internally NGINX groups by:

exact IP:port
wildcard IP:port (0.0.0.0)

Exact IP bindings take priority over wildcard bindings.
🎯 Important Precedence Rule

If both exist:

listen 80;                # wildcard
listen 127.0.0.1:80;      # specific

Then:

    Requests to 127.0.0.1:80 → prefer specific

    Requests to other IPs → wildcard

Specific beats wildcard.
